course: dictionary
lesson: 11911
title: Burdensome: heavy and hard
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Burdensome is a formal word, so you will see it in news, exam writing and grown-up talk. A burden is a heavy load, so burdensome means heavy to carry, or causing a lot of work, worry or difficulty. «The wet sack was burdensome, so Sam stopped every few steps.» The sack was too heavy to carry easily.
explain: The second meaning is about problems rather than weight. «The new rules were burdensome for small shops, with forms to fill in every week.» Nothing is physically heavy here, but the rules make life hard and tiring for the shops. The word tells you they are a weight on people.
explain: Check the context to see which meaning fits. Ask yourself: is something heavy, or is it giving someone a lot of trouble? In plain talk you could say «a pain», «hard» or «heavy» instead of burdensome, and see if the sentence still makes sense.
fact: Burdensome means heavy or hard to carry, or causing a lot of work, worry or difficulty.
fact: A burden is a heavy load, so burdensome describes something that feels like a weight.
fact: «Burdensome» is a formal word, so it is often used in news, official writing and exams.
fact: «Burdensome taxes» and «burdensome paperwork» are common examples: they make people's lives harder.
fact: In plain talk, you can say «a pain», «hard» or «heavy» instead of burdensome.
exercise: «The old sack was so burdensome that Sam had to stop every few steps.» What does burdensome mean here? | *Heavy and hard to carry | Bright and shiny | Full of cold water | Light and easy to lift
exercise: «The new rules were burdensome for small shops, with forms to fill in every week.» What does burdensome suggest? | *Causing a lot of work and difficulty | Brightly coloured | Very funny | Quick and easy to follow
exercise: Which plain word could you use instead of burdensome in «burdensome paperwork»? | Tasty | *A pain | Quiet | Sunny
test: «Carrying the heavy wet logs up the hill was burdensome.» What does burdensome mean? | Warm and dry | *Heavy and hard to carry | Blue | Easy and fun
test: Why might a writer choose burdensome instead of hard? | It is a word for food | It means very happy | *It is more formal and suggests a heavy weight | It is a shorter word
exam: «The burdensome taxes worried the family, because there was less money each month.» Which word is closest in meaning to burdensome? | Light | *Heavy | Cheap | Funny
